WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.830
When a family takes out a mortgage,
who ends up owning the loan?

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.710
Usually not the lender
that handed over the money.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.230
Let's follow one loan
and see where it goes.

00:00:10.988 --> 00:00:14.498
A family borrows $300,000 to buy a house.

00:00:14.808 --> 00:00:16.988
They'll pay it back month by month for

00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:19.218
30 years at a rate that never changes.

00:00:19.908 --> 00:00:22.328
The lender hands over the money
at the closing table, but the

00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:24.798
lender doesn't want to wait
30 years to get it back.

00:00:24.988 --> 00:00:27.738
So soon after closing, it sells the loan.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.958
The buyer is usually
Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac.

00:00:32.508 --> 00:00:36.458
Fannie Mae, for example,
would put the loan into a pool

00:00:36.508 --> 00:00:40.458
with thousands of others and turn
the pool into mortgage-backed bonds.

00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:45.858
Each bond pays its owner a small
share of every family's payments.

00:00:46.778 --> 00:00:49.518
Fannie Mae also promises the
bonds' owners that they'll

00:00:49.568 --> 00:00:52.308
get paid even if some
families stop paying.

00:00:52.838 --> 00:00:54.988
Because it's on the hook,
Fannie Mae has rules

00:00:55.038 --> 00:00:57.188
about the loans it will and won't buy.

00:00:57.648 --> 00:01:00.635
A limit on the loan's size,
extra protection, usually

00:01:00.685 --> 00:01:03.672
mortgage insurance when the
family puts down less than

00:01:03.722 --> 00:01:06.708
20%, and a proof of the family's income.

00:01:07.352 --> 00:01:09.442
Now let's watch one monthly payment.

00:01:09.832 --> 00:01:13.422
A company called a servicer
collects it and keeps a small fee.

00:01:13.952 --> 00:01:15.922
Fannie Mae keeps a fee for its guarantee.

00:01:15.972 --> 00:01:18.912
And the rest goes to the
bond's owner, a bank, a pension

00:01:18.962 --> 00:01:21.902
fund, or a government on
the other side of the world.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.650
Everyone in the machine gains.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.310
The lender gets its cash back
and lends to the next family.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.870
The investor gets steady
income from thousands of homes.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.910
And the family gets a fixed rate
for 30 years and a home to live in.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.400
In the 2000s, Wall Street
built a copy of this machine

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.390
for the loans Fannie Mae and
Freddie Mac wouldn't buy.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.910
Loans to borrowers with
weak credit and loans

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.950
with little or no proof of income.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.915
By 2005, Wall Street
was turning more loans

00:01:50.965 --> 00:01:53.480
into bonds than Fannie
and Freddie combined.

00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:57.833
The copy was missing the
guarantee, and with it the checks.

00:01:58.223 --> 00:02:01.373
Nobody had promised the bonds'
owners that they'd be paid.

00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:04.553
Instead, buyers trusted
ratings agencies, which were

00:02:04.603 --> 00:02:07.373
paid by the banks selling the bonds.

00:02:07.803 --> 00:02:09.453
In 2006 alone,

00:02:10.143 --> 00:02:13.293
rating agency Moody's
gave its top grade, AAA,

00:02:13.343 --> 00:02:16.493
to 30 mortgage bonds every working day.

00:02:17.051 --> 00:02:21.581
Now look at when each
link in the copy got paid.

00:02:21.631 --> 00:02:24.101
The broker who brought
the family to the lender

00:02:24.151 --> 00:02:26.621
when the loan was made made a bonus.

00:02:26.871 --> 00:02:29.901
The lender, when it sold
the loan, made commission.

00:02:30.211 --> 00:02:32.711
The Wall Street bank, when
it sold the bonds, and

00:02:32.761 --> 00:02:35.261
the rating agency, when it
graded them, each got paid.

00:02:35.891 --> 00:02:39.821
But the investor at the end
needed the family to keep paying.

00:02:40.713 --> 00:02:44.283
House prices peaked in 2006,
and then they fell hard.

00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:46.743
The riskiest loans went bad first.

00:02:47.193 --> 00:02:50.043
Lenders had promised those
loans met buyers' rules.

00:02:50.093 --> 00:02:53.123
So the ones that failed within
months were sent back to them.

00:02:53.953 --> 00:02:56.783
Payments stopped reaching the
investors, and the bonds lost value.

00:02:57.413 --> 00:03:01.503
Of the bonds Moody's graded
AAA in 2006, 83% were later cut

00:03:01.553 --> 00:03:05.643
to lower grades and may
not have paid back in full.

00:03:06.423 --> 00:03:09.436
The losses ran back up
the chain to the banks

00:03:09.486 --> 00:03:12.499
that held the bonds and
caused a series of cascading

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:15.563
failures, known today as
the 2008 financial crisis.

00:03:16.106 --> 00:03:19.496
So when you buy a house,
who actually owns it?

00:03:20.026 --> 00:03:20.736
You do.

00:03:21.026 --> 00:03:23.416
And the loan on it probably gets sold

00:03:23.466 --> 00:03:25.856
to an investor who you'll never meet.

00:03:25.906 --> 00:03:29.236
Most of the time, that
works fine for everyone.

00:03:29.506 --> 00:03:33.646
But it breaks down when nobody
in the chain is actually trusted

00:03:33.696 --> 00:03:37.836
or incentivized to check the loans
and make sure they're any good.
